# Cron alternative to contrib/wp-cli-update.timer.
#
# Install as /etc/cron.d/wp-cli-update (root-owned, mode 0644, no dot in the name).
# Prefer the systemd timer when both are available: it has RandomizedDelaySec and
# Persistent=true, and it records the last run in `systemctl status`.
#
# The scheduler's environment is minimal on purpose. WP_CLI_UPDATE_* variables are
# the only configuration the manager reads from the environment, and the config file
# is the better place for everything else.
#
# Cron does not retry and does not alert. The manager does both:
#   --retry 2 --retry-delay 30     retry a site that hit a transient failure
#   --max-failures 5               stop the fleet instead of writing 200 errors
#   --notify ... --notify-on failure   one alert when something went wrong
#
# MAILTO is honoured by cron itself: output is mailed only when there is output, and
# a clean run prints nothing to fd 1 (the summary goes to stderr, which cron mails).

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=""

# m   h  dom mon dow   user  command
  20  3  *   *   *     root  cd /opt/wp-cli-update && AUTO_DISCOVER=0 ./Bash_WP-CLI_Update.sh --full --backup db --skip-if-no-updates --retry 2 --max-failures 5 --min-free-mb 500 --report /var/log/wp-cli-update/last-run.json --notify-on failure >>/var/log/wp-cli-update/cron.out 2>&1

# Read-only inventory every ten minutes, for monitoring. It takes no lock and writes
# nothing, so it is safe to run while a maintenance run is in progress.
*/10 *  *  *  *     root  cd /opt/wp-cli-update && AUTO_DISCOVER=0 ./Bash_WP-CLI_Update.sh --health >/var/log/wp-cli-update/health.txt 2>/dev/null
